    passage: "How do migratory birds find their way across thousands of kilometers of featureless ocean or dark sky? The question has occupied biologists for a century, and the answer, it turns out, is that birds use not one compass but several, layered in a hierarchy. Experiments with caged birds have shown that many species orient by the stars, learning the night sky's rotation as nestlings and using it as a fixed map. Others detect the Earth's magnetic field through iron-rich cells in their beaks and eyes, sensing direction as a human feels a magnetic needle. And on cloudy days, birds fall back on landmarks and the position of the sun. Remarkably, the systems appear to back each other up: birds whose magnetic sense is disrupted in laboratory tests can still orient using the stars, and young birds making their first migration rely more heavily on the magnetic field, which is less affected by weather, than adults do. The redundancy, researchers argue, is precisely the point—a single compass would leave the birds helpless when conditions block it.",
