    passage: "The Dunning-Kruger effect, named for the psychologists who documented it in 1999, describes a peculiar double failure of self-assessment: people who perform poorly at a task tend to overestimate their ability, while experts tend to underestimate theirs. The explanation is intuitive once stated—the same knowledge required to do a task well is required to judge one's performance on it, so the unskilled lack the tools to recognize their own incompetence. The effect captured the public imagination and is now routinely cited to dismiss opponents' views. Psychologists caution, however, that the effect is often misrepresented. The original finding was modest—poor performers overestimated themselves relative to their peers, not absolutely—and replications have found the gap varies widely across tasks and populations. The effect is real, researchers conclude, but it is not a universal law of human nature, and using it as a casual insult has more to do with rhetoric than with science.",
